     "id": "design-07",
     "title": "Pairwise / combinatorial testing",
     "minutes": 11,
     "body": "When several independent inputs interact, full combinations explode. **Pairwise (all-pairs) testing** is a pragmatic compromise based on a striking empirical fact: most defects are triggered by a single input or a *pair* of inputs, rarely by three or more together.\n\n## The idea\n\nYou do not test every combination; you ensure that **every pair of values from any two parameters appears together at least once**.\n\nExample: 4 parameters, each with 3 values → 3⁴ = 81 full combinations. A pairwise set covers all value pairs in about 9-10 test cases. That is a ~8× reduction with very little loss of defect-finding power for interaction bugs.\n\n## Worked example: browser matrix\n\n| Param | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Browser | Chrome, Firefox, Safari |\n| OS | Windows, macOS, iOS |\n| User role | Guest, Member, Admin |\n\nFull = 3×3×3 = 27 combos. A valid pairwise set (9 rows):\n\n| # | Browser | OS | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Chrome | Windows | Guest |\n| 2 | Chrome | macOS | Member |\n| 3 | Chrome | iOS | Admin |\n| 4 | Firefox | Windows | Member |\n| 5 | Firefox | macOS | Admin |\n| 6 | Firefox | iOS | Guest |\n| 7 | Safari | Windows | Admin |\n| 8 | Safari | macOS | Guest |\n| 9 | Safari | iOS | Member |\n\nEvery browser×OS, browser×role, and OS×role pair appears. Generate these with a tool (e.g. `pairwise` tools, PICT, or an online all-pairs generator) rather than by hand — but know what the tool is doing and always **add explicit high-risk combinations** (e.g. Safari + iOS + Guest) even if the algorithm covers them.\n\n## When to use it — and when not to\n\nUse pairwise for **configuration/compatibility matrices**, feature-flag combinations, and form fields with many independent options. Do **not** use it to replace boundary analysis or decision tables for business rules: if three conditions genuinely interact (premium AND over $100 AND express), a decision table is the right tool. Pairwise assumes interactions are mostly pairwise; business logic often is not.\n\n> **From the desk:** 'it only breaks on Safari on the iPad for guests' is a *pairwise (actually 3-way)* interaction defect. You've troubleshot these; now you'll design a small matrix that catches them systematically.\n\n> **From the desk:** Compatibility tickets ('works in Chrome, not in Edge on the VPN') are interaction defects. Pairwise is the systematic version of your browser/OS/role troubleshooting grid.\n\n**Remember:** Cover every pair of parameter values; a handful of rows replace dozens. Keep decision tables for real business-rule interactions.",
