     "id": "sql-01",
     "title": "Tables, rows, keys and relationships",
     "minutes": 11,
     "body": "A relational database stores data in tables. Knowing the shape of the data is prerequisite to every verification query you'll write — and to understanding why a test failed.\n\n## The vocabulary\n\n| Concept | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Table / relation** | A named collection of rows (e.g. `test_cases`) |\n| **Column / field** | A typed attribute (`id`, `status`) |\n| **Row / record** | One entity instance |\n| **Primary key (PK)** | Unique identifier for a row (`id`) |\n| **Foreign key (FK)** | A column referencing another table's PK (`owner_id` → `testers.id`) |\n| **NULL** | *Unknown / no value* — not zero, not empty string, not false |\n| **Schema** | The structure: tables, columns, types, constraints |\n| **Index** | A structure that speeds up lookups |\n\nOur practice database (used by the runnable exercises) has three tables:\n\n```\ntesters(id, name, team, active)\ntest_cases(id, title, module, priority, status, owner_id)\ndefects(id, test_case_id, severity, status, assignee_id)\n```\n\n`test_cases.owner_id` → `testers.id`; `defects.test_case_id` → `test_cases.id`. That's a chain you can join across.\n\n## Why testers query databases\n\nThe UI can lie (or at least hide things). SQL lets you verify **persistence**: did the order row actually get created? is the `status` really 'Paid'? was the discount written to the DB, or only displayed? did the soft-delete flag get set? is the audit log complete? You also use SQL to **create test data**, **clean up** after tests, and **count** things for reports.\n\n> **From the desk:** you've probably already run a query to check whether a ticket's status in the UI matches the underlying record. That is exactly back-end verification.\n\n## NULL is special\n\n`NULL` means 'unknown'. Comparisons with it are not true or false — they are *unknown*, so `status = NULL` never matches anything. Use `IS NULL` / `IS NOT NULL`. Also `COUNT(*)` counts rows including NULLs, while `COUNT(column)` counts only non-NULL values in that column. This trips up almost everyone once.\n\n> **From the desk:** You already know a ticket's 'status' in the UI is just a view of a database field. SQL is how you check the field directly.\n\n**Remember:** Tables → rows → keys; FK links tables; NULL is unknown, use IS NULL; SQL verifies persistence, not just display.",
