     "id": "post-04",
     "title": "Designing API test cases",
     "minutes": 12,
     "body": "A collection is only as good as its test design. This lesson applies the Phase 1 techniques — equivalence partitioning, boundaries, decision tables, negative paths — to an API endpoint, producing a compact, defensible case list.\n\n## A worked case list for POST /api/orders\n\n| # | Case | Input | Expected |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Valid order | 1 item, qty 2, valid coupon | 201, total 108.00, `Location` header |\n| 2 | Missing required field | no `items` | 422 + error code |\n| 3 | Wrong type | `\"qty\": \"two\"` | 400, no write |\n| 4 | qty = 0 (boundary) | qty 0 | 422 |\n| 5 | qty = 1 (boundary) | qty 1 | 201 |\n| 6 | qty = max (boundary) | qty 999 | 201 or 422 per spec |\n| 7 | qty = max+1 | qty 1000 | 422 |\n| 8 | Negative qty | qty -1 | 422 |\n| 9 | Empty items array | `[]` | 422 |\n| 10 | Unknown coupon | `NOPE` | 422, total unchanged |\n| 11 | Expired coupon | `EXPIRED` | 422 |\n| 12 | Two coupons (decision rule) | both codes | documented behaviour |\n| 13 | Premium + >$100 + express (decision table) | flags | correct discount/shipping |\n| 14 | No auth | — | 401 |\n| 15 | Wrong role | — | 403 |\n| 16 | Duplicate POST | same body twice | 2 orders unless Idempotency-Key |\n| 17 | Injection string in `note` | `' OR 1=1 --` | escaped, no 500 |\n| 18 | Very long `note` | 10k chars | 422 or truncated per spec |\n| 19 | Follow-up GET | id from #1 | 200 with same values |\n| 20 | DELETE then GET | id from #1 | 204, then 404 |\n\nTwenty cases, each one line — and the collection becomes a **living test-design document**. Add the requirement/spec reference in each request description.\n\n## Organising by risk\n\nNot all 20 are equal. Mark the auth, payment/total, and duplicate-submit cases `High`; put them in a **Smoke** folder that runs on every build. Keep the full set as the regression collection. This is risk-based testing from the documentation course applied to APIs.\n\n## Data-driven runs\n\nThe Collection Runner can iterate a **CSV/JSON data file**: one row per case with `input` and `expectedStatus` columns, referenced as `{{input}}` / `{{expectedStatus}}` and `pm.iterationData.get('expectedStatus')` in tests. One request + 20 rows replaces 20 near-identical requests — a clean introduction to data-driven testing that you'll reuse in Playwright.\n\n> **From the desk:** Twenty lines in a table is a familiar escalation matrix. You've written these by hand; now they're executable requests with assertions.\n\n**Remember:** Apply EP/BVA/decision tables/negative/auth/idempotency per endpoint; tag High-risk into a smoke folder; drive repeats with a data file.",
     "exercises": [
      {
       "type": "mcq",
       "prompt": "Which case is a boundary value for a quantity field capped at 999?",
       "options": [
        "qty 500",
        "qty 999 and qty 1000",
        "qty 2",
        "qty -999"
       ],
       "answer": 1
      },
      {
       "type": "mcq",
       "prompt": "What belongs in a 'Smoke' folder for an orders API?",
       "options": [
        "All 20 cases",
        "High-risk auth, total, and duplicate-submit cases that run every build",
        "Only the slowest cases",
        "Only the UI tests"
       ],
       "answer": 1
      },
      {
       "type": "mcq",
       "prompt": "The Collection Runner data file mainly enables:",
       "options": [
        "Faster internet",
        "Data-driven testing — one request, many input/expected rows",
        "Automatic screenshots",
        "Writing SQL"
       ],
       "answer": 1
      }
     ]
