     "id": "nf-04",
     "title": "Usability, compatibility and mobile",
     "minutes": 11,
     "body": "A feature can be functionally correct and still fail users. This lesson covers the human-facing checks and the device/browser matrix, and how to keep them tractable with risk-based selection.\n\n## Usability checks\n\n- **Clarity**: does the user know what to do next? are labels, button text and error messages plain and actionable ('Coupon SAVE10 is not valid' vs 'Error 42')?\n- **Feedback**: loading indicators, success confirmation, disabled states, progress on long operations.\n- **Recovery**: can the user undo, go back, correct an error without losing everything?\n- **Consistency**: same words, colours, and patterns across the app.\n- **Cognitive load**: minimal steps, sensible defaults, no unnecessary fields.\n- **Error prevention**: confirmations for destructive actions, sensible input constraints.\n\nUsability findings are often **'works as designed'** — that's fine. Log them as *usability improvements* with user impact, not as functional defects. This is exactly the 'absence-of-errors fallacy' from the principles: bug-free can still be bad.\n\n## Compatibility matrix (kept sane)\n\nDimensions: browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari), OS (Windows, macOS, iOS, Android), viewport/device, and version. Full coverage exploded in the pairwise lesson — use **pairwise/all-pairs** to build a small matrix, then **add explicit high-risk rows** (your analytics' top 3 real user configurations). Prioritise by **actual usage data**, not by what the team happens to have installed.\n\nCheck: layout/overflow, date & number formats, fonts, touch targets, file inputs, print, zoom, and browser-specific JS/CSS support. Note which combinations are explicitly **out of scope** in the test plan.\n\n## Mobile-specific risks\n\nSmall viewports and touch (no hover), on-screen keyboard covering inputs, orientation change, interrupted network (tube/tunnel), low battery/low memory, app backgrounding and resume, push notifications, permission prompts (camera/location), OS-level font scaling, and flaky connectivity. **Real devices** beat emulators for these; use BrowserStack / Sauce Labs or a small physical device library when available. Also test **responsive breakpoints** and 200% zoom — that overlaps with accessibility.\n\n> **From the desk:** Desktop thin-client vs laptop vs phone reproduces are compatibility tickets you've triaged. A small, evidence-based device matrix turns that reactive work into planned coverage.\n\n**Remember:** Usability = clarity, feedback, recovery, consistency; build a pairwise + usage-driven compatibility matrix; test mobile interruptions and permissions on real devices.",
